## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-cloud multi-tenant infra from a single module, balancing reuse with isolation. Targets provider aliasing, per-tenant tagging, and policy-driven gates.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliasing and for_each
- Cross-cloud module composition
- Policy-as-code (OPA/Vault) for prod changes
- CI gates around per-tenant plan validation

## Code Example

```hcl
# skeleton: tenants map and module per-tenant
variable "tenants" {
  type = map(object({
    cloud  = string
    region = string
  }))
}

module "tenant" {
  for_each = var.tenants
  source   = "./modules/tenant"

  tenant_name = each.key
  cloud       = each.value.cloud
  region      = each.value.region

  # pass per-tenant providers (pseudo-syntax for illustration)
  providers = {
    aws    = aws.${each.value.cloud == "aws" ? "alias" : "default"}
    google = google.${each.value.cloud == "gcp" ? "alias" : "default"}
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test drift with cross-cloud resources and ensure idempotence? 
- What are trade-offs between centralized tenants state vs per-tenant backends?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical multi-cloud Terraform design, policy-as-code, drift handling, and test automation at scale—core for Oracle, Tesla, and LinkedIn-like environments.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliasing for multi-region/multi-cloud setups
- Wrapper modules to standardize tagging and policy constraints
- Terraform Cloud/Sentinel or OPA for governance
- Drift detection workflows and isolated drift environments
- End-to-end testing with Terratest or kitchen-terraform

## Code Example

```hcl
# Wrapper module injecting standard tags
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "cost_center" { type = string }
variable "tags" { type = map(string) }

module "service" {
  source = "../aws-azure-base"
  providers = {
    aws   = aws.us_east_1
    aws_eu = aws.eu_west_1
    azurerm = azurerm.westus2
  }
  tags = merge(var.tags, {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you structure a Sentinel policy to enforce tag requirements and deny secrets in state?
- How would you simulate drift in tests and verify policy compliance across clouds?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to implement basic policy enforcement in Terraform using native provider features and CI/CD gates.

## Key Concepts
- AWS provider default_tags for universal tag propagation across all resources
- Wrapper module to enforce consistent environment context
- Per-environment backend configuration in S3
- GitHub Actions gate using terraform plan output to detect policy violations

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = var.region
  default_tags {
    tags = {
      Environment = var.environment
      CostCenter  = var.cost_center
      Owner       = var.owner
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a cloud-agnostic Terraform module with clean separation of concerns, provider aliasing, and robust gating to prevent drift or accidental exposure.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliases and multi-cloud orchestration
- Module abstraction over provider-specific resources
- Per-environment backends and key management
- CI plan gating with JSON plan validation
- Basic test coverage via Terratest for existence and config

## Code Example

```terraform
# Root usage
variable "cloud" { type = string }
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool }
variable "public_read" { type = bool }
variable "environment" { type = string }

provider "aws" { alias = "aws" region = "us-east-1" }
provider "google" { alias = "google" project = var.environment }
provider "azurerm" { alias = "azurerm" features = {} }

module "object_store" {
  source = "./modules/object_store"

  cloud       = var.cloud
  bucket_name = var.bucket_name
  versioning  = var.versioning
  public_read = var.public_read
  environment = var.environment
}
```

```terraform
# modules/object_store/main.tf (skeleton)
variable "cloud" { type = string }
variable "bucket_name" { type = string }
variable "versioning" { type = bool }
variable "public_read" { type = bool }
variable "environment" { type = string }

provider "aws" { alias = "aws" }
provider "google" { alias = "google" }
provider "azurerm" { alias = "azurerm" }

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  count = var.cloud == "aws" ? 1 : 0
  bucket = var.bucket_name
  acl    = var.public_read ? "public-read" : "private"

  versioning {
    enabled = var.versioning
  }
}

# google and azurerm blocks would mirror the above with appropriate attributes
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to support policy-driven controls (e.g., CFRs) across clouds?
- How would you test drift between environments and prevent accidental exposure in prod?
